\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Task 1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1. ?- house = house.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 true, bc The terms can be unified because they are identical atoms.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2. ?- house = abc.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 false, bc  The terms cannot be unified because they are two distinct, non-identical atoms.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3. ?- edge(a,b) = edge(C,b).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 	\'95
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2  C = a, bc The terms share the same functor (edge) and arity (2). The second arguments (b) match, and the variable C is bound to the atom a.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 4. ?- edge(a,d) = edge(C,B).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2  C = a, B = d, bcBoth terms have the same functor and arity. Variable C binds to atom a, and variable B binds to atom d.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 5. ?- edge(C,B) = edge(a,d).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 C = a, B = d, bc Unification is symmetric. The variables on the left (C and B) unify with the atoms on the right (a and d).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 6. ?- edge(A,f(g,h)) = edge(a,B).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2  A = a, B = f(g,h), bc Both terms share functor and arity. The first argument binds variable A to atom a, and the second argument binds variable B to the compound term f(g,h).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 7. ?- edge(A,A) = edge(b,c).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 false, bc Unification fails. Variable A cannot be bound to two different constants (b and c) simultaneously ($b \\neq c$).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 8. ?- edge(A,A) = edge(b,b).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2  A = b, bc Variable A unifies with atom b in the first argument, which consistently satisfies the second argument b.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 9. ?- A = B, B = f.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A = f, B = f, bc  First, variable A unifies with variable B (aliasing them). Then B binds to atom f, which automatically instantiates A to f as well.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 10. ?- A = B, B = f, A = e.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 false, bc A and B both bind to f. The final goal attempts to unify A (now f) with e. Since f and e are different atoms, unification fails\
}